# Cross-platform/multi-platform docker images
Docker images for different and/or multiple architectures may be built using
`sudo make docker-multiarch`.

# Single-platform image for the current platform (compatibility)
As usual: `docker build .`

# Basic multi-platform build
Publish to your private docker registry, using the default set of platforms:

`sudo make docker-multiarch REGISTRY=docker.example.com`

# Specify your list of platforms
`sudo make docker-multiarch REGISTRY=docker.example.com PLATFORMS=linux/arm/v7`

For details on the supported platforms, see `./fs_overlay/bin/archname`.

# Set-up
Docker with the `buildx` plugin is required. Docker Desktop includes it on
macOS. The Makefile uses Docker Desktop's local `desktop-linux` context by
default and creates a dedicated builder named `https-portal-multiarch`.
QEMU/binfmt is installed in Docker Desktop's Linux VM automatically, allowing
ARM-based Macs to build the x86 images as well as the ARM images.

To use another Docker host, override the context and, optionally, builder name:

`make docker-multiarch DOCKER_CONTEXT=default BUILDER=https-portal-multiarch`

Build and push the multi-platform images with:

`make docker-multiarch-push`
